views {

    // 'index' is reserved: LikeC4 generates its own landing view if no view claims
    // that id, so this one keeps it and carries its level in the title alone.
    view index {
        title '[C1] MicroWorld - System Context'
        description 'Why MicroWorld exists. One application reaching three hardware targets is the point of the product, and the fan-out states it by shape rather than by adjective. Devices exchange messages, not bytes, so the developer never writes link-level code. The UE5 familiarity claim is a property of the API, so it lives in the product description rather than pretending to be a relationship.'
        include developer
        include deviceFirmware
        include microworld
        include esp32Device
        include picoDevice
        include developmentPc
        autoLayout TopBottom
    }

    view c2Systems {
        title '[C2] MicroWorld'
        description 'Six systems, each named for the responsibility it owns and carrying a glyph of what it does. The layout stacks them in dependency order - composition on top, foundation at the bottom - so no colour key is needed for either. Three of these boundaries are decisions rather than groupings, and each answers a question someone will ask again. Engine is one system because Object owns identity and Engine owns the lifetime built on it, and neither is a responsibility anything wants alone. Transport holds every medium because a named one - Wi-Fi, a wire, a radio - only satisfies the device contract and declares nothing outward, which makes it an implementation. And Engine and Transport have no edge between them, nor does any system join them: that total absence is the most important rule in the model, and the play-system turn points in Core are what let a service extend a loop it cannot see. Messaging System sits beside them on Core rather than between them, with two ways in - one through Application alone, one through Networking - because local actor messaging needs neither Networking nor Transport. Messaging System and Networking are both IPlaySystem implementations a composition root installs, and neither depends on Engine. So is a transport device, which is why Application has an edge to Transport as well: a callback cannot fire itself, so whoever owns a device installs it - an application holding one directly pumps it in its own frame loop, Networking pumps the ones it owns, and never both for the same device. Delivery guarantees live in Networking rather than below it: Transport answers that it accepted bytes, never that they arrived, because an acknowledgement is a message from the peer and a byte layer that reads one has stopped carrying payload opaquely. Messaging only declares whether a channel wants reliable delivery, which costs nothing when there is no wire. Splitting an oversized message lands in Networking too - a device publishes the largest payload it can take and refuses more. Every dependency label names what actually crosses it.'
        include microworld
        include microworld.core
        include espIdf
        include hostOs
        include microworld.engine
        include microworld.messagingSystem
        include microworld.transport
        include microworld.app
        include microworld.networking
        autoLayout TopBottom
    }

    view c3Engine of microworld.engine {
        title '[C3] Engine'
        description 'C3, because the deferred spawn barrier and generation-checked handles are the two things a new contributor reliably gets wrong. The identity-versus-lifetime split is deliberately not repeated on these seven children - it is a C2 fact and the C2 subtitle carries it.'
        include *
        autoLayout TopBottom
    }

    view c3Transport of microworld.transport {
        title '[C3] Transport'
        description 'Flat on purpose. An application holds a medium device directly and calls the same three operations on whichever one it picked - configure it, send to an address, subscribe for arrivals - so there is no layer above the devices and nothing hides which medium is in use. Choosing the medium is the point, which is why the contract is uniform in its data path and deliberately not in Setup: SendDataTo reads identically everywhere, while an SSID and a baud rate have nothing in common. Uniform is the shape and not the meaning - each medium reads the address its own way, and a point-to-point one ignores it. Subscribing does not make bytes appear either: a device is also a play system, and whoever owns it installs it. Three things are then deliberately absent. Send never blocks, so it answers that it had no room rather than waiting. Nothing here sequences, acknowledges or retries, because Send answers acceptance and never delivery. And nothing here splits: a device publishes the largest payload it can take and refuses anything longer. All three sit above this level in Networking, and keeping them out is what keeps a device a device. The frame codec has exactly two edges, and the two missing ones are the point of the view - Wi-Fi and Bluetooth deliver bounded datagrams, while radio and wired media receive a continuous stream and must recover message boundaries. Each device is one medium, not one class: the three wired buses share a box because nothing they differ in - wiring, who clocks the line, how a peer is named - changes the shape they realise. Below the media sit the platform implementations, drawn outside the product boundary because we do not own them: a medium here wraps somebody elses radio or bus driver, it does not invent one. Read the arrows into them and you get which board can do what - ESP-IDF realises every medium, a development host realises only Wi-Fi, and the RP2040 reaches only LoRa. LoRa is the one medium whose arrows read differently: it is not realised by a platform at all, it merely takes a byte stream from one, because the E32 protocol is ours and portable while the wire underneath is not. Bluetooth has no realisation yet and appears anyway, because the architecture decides which media exist and the code follows.'
        include *
        include espIdf
        include picoSdk
        include hostOs
        autoLayout TopBottom
    }

}
